@@ -2184,8 +2184,8 @@
 title = "The verdict record: what carries a governing verdict, and what binds it to the tree it judged"
 module = "project-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/verdict.py"
 code_symbol = "RECORD_PREFIXES/fold_listing/tree_identity/refresh_subject/refresh_attestation/work_tip/governing_rev/governing_identity/format_trailer/parse_trailer/round_file/session_log/branch_paths/logged_rounds/round_entries/branch_entries/declared_phases/phases_owed/round_count/format_branch_trailer/branch_trailers"
-detail = """The reading behind the merge seam's verdict rung, sited below both of its readers. tree_identity folds `git ls-tree -r -z` with the RECORD prefixes dropped - docs/reviews/, docs/log.d/, docs/iteration/ - into a SHA-256 over the surviving entries, which is what makes "the tree a verdict judged" a value two independent readers can compare; the digest is 64 hex deliberately, so it cannot be mistaken for the git tree object id the Bar-Green trailer carries. -z is the ENCODING BOUNDARY and not a formatting choice: git quotes any path holding a non-ASCII or special character, and a quoted path defeats the record-prefix test, so fold_listing takes raw byte entries and never decodes them or sees git's display encoding. What CANNOT invalidate a verdict has two halves and this module owns both: the record PATHS, and the station's refresh COMMIT. refresh_attestation verifies a Bar-Green: tree=<40 hex> work=<40 hex> trailer against the commit's own tree, its first parent and this branch's refresh subject; the peel is then written TWICE, on purpose, because its two callers can afford different answers: work_tip peels only a refresh sitting on the tip, at the work sha the refresh itself recorded, because it feeds a reset --hard where peeling one commit too far destroys committed work; governing_rev walks THROUGH any commit whose non-record identity equals its first parent's, to reach a refresh it would otherwise hide, which a read-only reader can do safely. Merging the two is a defect in either direction, and leaving them merged was one: while the identity used the tip-only peel, a single telemetry commit - a record path the fold exists to ignore - moved the identity by burying the refresh under it, so an approval served before the refresh named a tree nothing governed and both readers agreed on the wrong answer. The walk's step condition is that sentence itself - a commit that cannot invalidate a verdict - and not a proxy for it, because the proxy was the same defect one shape further on: a predicate CLASSIFYING the paths a commit touched must stop at every commit whose paths it cannot read, and an attestation riding unchanged bookkeeping commits EMPTY, so the commit RECORDING an approval buried the refresh. Asking the identity question directly makes the empty carrier, the merge commit and the quoted-path trap unrepresentable together, since no path is classified in the walk at all. governing_identity composes governing_rev with the fold, so the merge slot and the loop's review-owed derivation are HANDED one answer rather than each choosing the rev it measures at; it takes the REV as an optional argument, because the readers that ask about a COMMIT rather than about the branch - the sha a round file cites, and the commit an attestation rides - must be answered by the same composition, and a raw fold there is that composition with the peel left out. Leaving it out was the same defect once more: a round drawn AFTER a refresh names the post-refresh tree while the gate governs by the peeled one, so the two were permanently unequal and no commit on the branch could make them agree - the round invisible to both readers, and an identical one re-drawn every tick. fold_listing is pure over the entries, so the one rule that decides whether two trees are the same work is exercisable without a repository. logged_rounds is the provenance join: a file under docs/reviews/ is a ROUND only when the coordinator's own committed session log for that (train, ordinal) declares a REVIEW phase, so an implementer-authored file in the review path is not evidence. round_entries restricts the rounds to those whose reviewed commit GOVERNS the identity under judgement - through governing_identity at that commit, never the bare fold - and hands the phase/ordinal/verdict triples to the latest-per-phase rule the scoring layer already owned; the parse function is INJECTED rather than imported, so this leaf keeps no edge back into scoring. declared_phases and phases_owed carry the COUNT dimension of the same question the identity carries the WHICH-TREE dimension of, and they are here for the same reason: the merge slot was taught to demand every phase the review_rounds dial declares (round 022 - the parsed count had been collapsed to a boolean, so a lone REVIEW-A cleared a policy of 2) while agent_loop.review_owed_by_evidence still read any verdict at this tree as the round being served, and the review phase queue is in-memory run state. A run that died between REVIEW-A and REVIEW-B therefore left a lane that scheduled nothing and a gate that refused the merge for the phase nobody would draw. declared_phases is the span both readers slice - the clamp is its whole content, since a NEGATIVE policy slices REVIEW_PHASES from the END and an over-dialled one asks for a phase no reviewer can be routed to. phases_owed answers with the MISSING PHASES rather than yes/no, which is what lets the resume redraw only those: redrawing a phase already served at this identity would re-run a reviewer that already spoke, and a dissent so redrawn reads to _round_refusal as a reroll-until-green. It asks whether a phase was DRAWN and not whether it approved, so it is deliberately weaker than the gate's test on exactly one class - a present but UNPARSEABLE verdict, which must not be redrawn (the double-round class) and must not clear the gate either. round_count gives `rounds=<N>` its evidence-derived scope: completed review cycles represented at ONE governing tree, computed as the largest per-phase entry count because one cycle may carry REVIEW-A and REVIEW-B; changing the work changes the identity and starts this count over. branch_trailers reads the Review-Verdict: APPROVE|CHANGES-REQUESTED rounds=<N> tree=<64 hex> machine half off the branch's own commits, verified against the identity the carrying commit GOVERNS - the same composition again, exactly as the bar attestation is verified against its own tree - and returns per tree the ORDERED SEQUENCE of attestations rather than one: a re-drawn round stamps a second trailer at the same tree, and a map fed from newest-first git log handed its reader the superseded tally. Off git every reader answers None and says nothing about what that means; the two callers declare opposite-looking fail directions that are the same direction - refuse the merge, assume a round is owed."""
-rationale = """The gate and the loop ask one question from two sides - may this merge, and does this lane still owe a round - so one definition below both readers prevents process-record commits or unlogged files from producing different answers. The trailer is deliberately NOT an accept path and the module says so: anyone with commit access can stamp one on their own commit and the self-verification will pass, because it is their tree - what a session cannot forge is the coordinator's committed session log, so the round evidence decides and the trailer is read as a cross-check that refuses when either its verdict word or its evidence-derived round count contradicts the rounds. Binding by identity rather than by ordering makes staleness the direct question "does this still describe the thing", with no last-one-wins rule that could promote an approval for different work."""
+detail = """tree_identity folds raw NUL-delimited `git ls-tree -r -z` entries after dropping docs/reviews/, docs/log.d/ and docs/iteration/, and returns a 64-hex SHA-256 identity. fold_listing accepts bytes and preserves every path byte so display quoting or decoding cannot merge distinct work trees. refresh_attestation validates a station refresh from its subject, first parent, commit tree and Bar-Green trailer. work_tip peels only a verified refresh at the tip for the reset path; governing_rev is read-only and walks across commits whose non-record identity equals their first parent's until it can peel a verified refresh. governing_identity composes governing_rev and tree_identity for HEAD or an explicit revision, including a round's reviewed commit and a trailer's carrier. logged_rounds joins branch-scoped round files to coordinator session logs by train, ordinal and review phase. round_entries retains only rounds whose reviewed revision governs the identity under judgement and emits phase, ordinal and verdict entries through an injected parser. declared_phases clamps the configured independent-review count to the available review phases. phases_owed returns declared phases with no drawn entry at the governing identity, independent of whether a drawn verdict parses or approves. round_count returns the largest per-phase entry count at one governing identity, so a multi-phase review cycle counts once and changed work starts a new count. format_trailer and parse_trailer carry `Review-Verdict: APPROVE|CHANGES-REQUESTED rounds=<N> tree=<64 hex>`. branch_trailers verifies each carrier against its governing identity and returns the ordered attestation sequence per tree. Readers treat unavailable repository answers according to their own fail-closed contract."""
+rationale = """The merge gate asks whether a lane may merge while the loop asks which review phase the lane still owes. A shared identity, evidence join, phase span and count keep both answers on the same tree and policy. Logged round evidence is authoritative because its coordinator session establishes reviewer provenance; the self-verifying trailer is only a cross-check and cannot create an approval. Identity binding answers staleness directly and prevents ordering from promoting a verdict for different work. Separate read-only and reset peels protect both contracts: the reader may traverse identity-preserving commits, while the destructive reset path may remove only the verified refresh at its tip."""
